{
  "title": "The Water Cycle",
  "subject": "geography",
  "environment": "studio",
  "summary": "Water evaporates from the ocean, rises and condenses into a cloud, falls as rain over land, then runs off back into the ocean. The arrows make it a continuous cycle rather than a one-way journey.",
  "mode": "illustration",
  "physics": "none",
  "sourceReading": "The diagram is titled The water cycle. At upper left is a gold Sun circle. A curved teal arrow labelled Evaporation rises from a blue Ocean rectangle at lower left to a pale cloud at top centre labelled Condensation. A second curved teal arrow falls from the cloud past three small blue triangles labelled Rain onto a green triangular Land at lower right. A thick blue band runs from the land toward the ocean, with a straight left-pointing teal arrow beneath it labelled Runoff. No numerical values are shown.",
  "assumptions": [
    "Depth, thickness and 3D forms are inferred: the flat ocean rectangle and land triangle are shown as a water block and a raised mountain so the cycle can be viewed in space.",
    "Arrow shafts are represented by curved tubes and cone heads; their exact curvature and position approximate the arrows in the diagram.",
    "Small vapour spheres and three rain drops are illustrative additions that make the labelled evaporation and rain stages visible; they are not drawn as separate objects in the source.",
    "Motion is illustrative, not a physical simulation of evaporation rates, rainfall, or runoff speed.",
    "Sizes and positions are not to scale; the sun, cloud, ocean and land are compressed into one teaching scene."
  ],
